1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,820
On the surface of Venus, it's about four
hundred and sixty-five degrees Celsius.

2
00:00:05,515 --> 00:00:07,175
Hot enough to melt lead.

3
00:00:07,755 --> 00:00:10,315
Day and night,
at the poles and at the equator.

4
00:00:10,989 --> 00:00:14,169
The air presses down
ninety times harder than on Earth.

5
00:00:14,249 --> 00:00:17,329
Like being nearly a kilometre
under the sea.

6
00:00:17,956 --> 00:00:23,696
In 1982, a Soviet lander called Venera 13
touched down on that surface.

7
00:00:24,431 --> 00:00:26,831
It was built to survive for half an hour.

8
00:00:27,251 --> 00:00:31,191
It lasted a little over two hours.
Then it went silent.

9
00:00:32,555 --> 00:00:36,275
And yet, some engineers at NASA argue
that the most Earth-like conditions

10
00:00:36,505 --> 00:00:39,575
anywhere beyond our own planet
are right here.

11
00:00:40,117 --> 00:00:44,017
Not on the surface.
Fifty kilometres above it.

12
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:47,447
Most plans to live on another planet
are about Mars.

13
00:00:47,747 --> 00:00:50,167
So let's do something a little unusual.

14
00:00:50,867 --> 00:00:54,307
Let's keep score. Venus against Mars.

15
00:00:56,677 --> 00:00:58,797
First, where exactly would we live?

16
00:00:59,037 --> 00:01:02,657
As you rise from the surface,
the air gets thinner and cooler.

17
00:01:03,325 --> 00:01:06,065
At fifty kilometres,
the pressure is almost exactly

18
00:01:06,295 --> 00:01:08,705
what you feel at sea level on Earth.

19
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:12,545
But the temperature
is still about seventy-five degrees.

20
00:01:13,364 --> 00:01:17,564
Go five kilometres higher,
and it drops to about room temperature.

21
00:01:18,345 --> 00:01:21,245
Now the pressure is about half
of what we're used to.

22
00:01:21,885 --> 00:01:24,785
Roughly what climbers feel
at Everest Base Camp.

23
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:27,879
So there's no single perfect altitude.

24
00:01:28,579 --> 00:01:32,179
There's a band, a few kilometres thick,
where you choose your compromise.

25
00:01:33,112 --> 00:01:35,892
Inside that band,
you wouldn't need a pressure suit.

26
00:01:36,512 --> 00:01:38,732
You wouldn't freeze,
and you wouldn't boil.

27
00:01:39,439 --> 00:01:41,719
You'd still need a sealed mask
with your own air supply,

28
00:01:41,949 --> 00:01:43,999
because the atmosphere is carbon dioxide.

29
00:01:44,229 --> 00:01:48,659
And protection from the clouds,
for reasons we'll get to.

30
00:01:50,939 --> 00:01:53,439
So, point one. Gravity.

31
00:01:53,519 --> 00:01:56,179
Venus is almost the same size as Earth,

32
00:01:56,259 --> 00:01:58,759
and its gravity is about ninety percent
of ours.

33
00:01:59,614 --> 00:02:02,333
On Mars, it's thirty-eight percent.

34
00:02:02,633 --> 00:02:05,413
Nobody has ever lived
in gravity like that.

35
00:02:05,993 --> 00:02:08,333
We don't know what years of it would do
to bones,

36
00:02:08,413 --> 00:02:11,333
to muscles,
or to a child growing up there.

37
00:02:11,925 --> 00:02:14,845
On Venus, you'd weigh nearly
as much as you do at home.

38
00:02:15,565 --> 00:02:17,025
Venus one, Mars nil.

39
00:02:19,485 --> 00:02:21,025
Point two. Radiation.

40
00:02:21,705 --> 00:02:24,525
Venus has no magnetic field to protect it.

41
00:02:24,985 --> 00:02:26,265
Neither does Mars.

42
00:02:26,873 --> 00:02:30,673
But at fifty kilometres on Venus,
there is more air above your head

43
00:02:30,903 --> 00:02:33,033
than there is above a beach on Earth.

44
00:02:33,646 --> 00:02:37,076
About a kilogram of air
on every square centimetre.

45
00:02:37,546 --> 00:02:40,666
Enough to block much of the radiation
coming in from space.

46
00:02:41,467 --> 00:02:44,267
On Mars, there's less than two percent
of that.

47
00:02:44,647 --> 00:02:48,607
That's why many plans for Mars
bury their habitats under soil.

48
00:02:49,245 --> 00:02:51,145
Venus two, Mars nil.

49
00:02:53,145 --> 00:02:55,785
Point three, and this is the strange one.

50
00:02:56,305 --> 00:02:58,345
How does a city stay up in the sky?

51
00:02:59,088 --> 00:03:01,668
On Earth, a balloon rises
when it's filled with a gas

52
00:03:01,898 --> 00:03:04,128
that's lighter than the air around it.

53
00:03:04,788 --> 00:03:06,568
Helium, or hydrogen.

54
00:03:07,007 --> 00:03:10,767
But the atmosphere of Venus
is almost entirely carbon dioxide.

55
00:03:11,487 --> 00:03:14,007
And carbon dioxide is heavy.

56
00:03:14,593 --> 00:03:18,753
About half as heavy again
as the nitrogen and oxygen we breathe.

57
00:03:19,025 --> 00:03:22,745
Which means that on Venus,
the air we breathe is a lifting gas.

58
00:03:23,495 --> 00:03:27,635
Fill a giant envelope with ordinary
breathable air, and it floats.

59
00:03:28,433 --> 00:03:32,733
At fifty kilometres, every cubic metre
of it lifts about half a kilogram.

60
00:03:32,813 --> 00:03:35,913
About half of what helium lifts on Earth.

61
00:03:35,993 --> 00:03:39,673
Higher up, where it's cooler,
it's closer to a quarter.

62
00:03:40,416 --> 00:03:41,956
Not much on its own.

63
00:03:42,556 --> 00:03:46,396
But build it large enough, and it can
carry the people living inside it.

64
00:03:46,914 --> 00:03:50,114
In fact, they could live
inside the lifting gas itself.

65
00:03:50,754 --> 00:03:53,874
The whole balloon would be
one enormous room full of air.

66
00:03:54,457 --> 00:03:55,777
And there's a bonus.

67
00:03:56,517 --> 00:03:59,377
The city would keep its air
at the same pressure as outside.

68
00:04:00,230 --> 00:04:03,070
Higher up, that's only about half
of Earth's pressure.

69
00:04:03,990 --> 00:04:05,350
So the mix would get extra oxygen,

70
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:09,370
and each breath would carry as much
as it does at sea level.

71
00:04:09,885 --> 00:04:14,965
With the same pressure inside and out, the
envelope doesn't have to hold anything in.

72
00:04:15,507 --> 00:04:18,787
Punch a hole in it,
and there's no explosive decompression.

73
00:04:19,207 --> 00:04:23,547
The air just seeps out, slowly,
and carbon dioxide seeps in.

74
00:04:24,053 --> 00:04:27,173
One NASA engineer estimated
that even a large rip

75
00:04:27,403 --> 00:04:31,593
would take thousands of hours
to leak a significant amount of air.

76
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:36,210
On Mars, the air outside is less than one
percent of the pressure on Earth.

77
00:04:36,870 --> 00:04:40,070
A hole means your air
is escaping into almost nothing.

78
00:04:41,130 --> 00:04:42,710
Venus three, Mars nil.

79
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:47,280
And this isn't entirely theoretical.

80
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:53,060
In 1985, two Soviet spacecraft,
Vega 1 and Vega 2,

81
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:55,740
flew past Venus
on their way to Halley's Comet.

82
00:04:56,479 --> 00:04:58,939
Each of them dropped a balloon
into the clouds.

83
00:04:59,797 --> 00:05:02,277
They floated about fifty-four
kilometres up,

84
00:05:02,357 --> 00:05:05,897
for roughly two days,
radioing data back to Earth.

85
00:05:06,506 --> 00:05:08,186
In that time, the winds carried them

86
00:05:08,416 --> 00:05:11,306
at nearly two hundred and fifty
kilometres an hour,

87
00:05:11,406 --> 00:05:13,286
a third of the way around the planet.

88
00:05:14,026 --> 00:05:16,366
They were small,
and they were filled with helium.

89
00:05:16,626 --> 00:05:18,986
But they proved something important.

90
00:05:19,863 --> 00:05:22,043
We have already flown balloons on Venus.

91
00:05:22,683 --> 00:05:24,343
We just haven't sent people.

92
00:05:26,673 --> 00:05:29,513
NASA engineers have thought
about that next step.

93
00:05:30,293 --> 00:05:32,413
A study called HAVOC
sketched a crewed airship

94
00:05:32,643 --> 00:05:34,773
a hundred and twenty-nine metres long.

95
00:05:35,368 --> 00:05:40,268
Two astronauts would live on board for a
month, fifty kilometres above the surface.

96
00:05:40,968 --> 00:05:42,268
The airship would have to unfold

97
00:05:42,498 --> 00:05:46,048
and inflate while it was still falling
through the atmosphere.

98
00:05:46,845 --> 00:05:49,185
It was a study, never an approved mission.

99
00:05:49,265 --> 00:05:53,405
But it showed that the idea can be worked
through with real engineering.

100
00:05:56,135 --> 00:05:57,875
So, three nil to Venus.

101
00:05:58,515 --> 00:06:00,595
Which raises an awkward question.

102
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:04,507
If Venus wins this easily,
why is almost every plan

103
00:06:04,737 --> 00:06:06,367
you've ever heard of about Mars?

104
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:10,587
Because so far, we've only counted
what Venus is good at.

105
00:06:13,277 --> 00:06:16,237
Point four. The clouds.
They aren't made of water.

106
00:06:16,467 --> 00:06:20,597
They're made of droplets
of concentrated sulphuric acid.

107
00:06:20,990 --> 00:06:23,910
And our comfortable band
sits right among them.

108
00:06:24,457 --> 00:06:26,957
Metal corrodes. Seals fail.

109
00:06:27,037 --> 00:06:30,297
Anything that isn't protected
slowly gets eaten.

110
00:06:30,977 --> 00:06:34,437
The good news is that we know
materials that survive acid.

111
00:06:35,177 --> 00:06:38,437
Chemists have kept sulphuric acid
in glass for centuries.

112
00:06:39,224 --> 00:06:42,504
In one NASA test,
a thin film of Teflon spent a month

113
00:06:42,734 --> 00:06:45,024
in concentrated sulphuric acid.

114
00:06:45,824 --> 00:06:50,584
It still let through about ninety percent
of the light, as much as on day one.

115
00:06:51,098 --> 00:06:55,438
A sample of ordinary PVC
started turning brown within an hour.

116
00:06:55,973 --> 00:06:59,413
But a film in a lab
is not a city in a storm, for decades.

117
00:07:00,333 --> 00:07:02,033
It would need constant repair.

118
00:07:02,855 --> 00:07:06,735
Mars has its problems, but its atmosphere
won't dissolve your house.

119
00:07:07,275 --> 00:07:09,175
Venus three, Mars one.

120
00:07:11,115 --> 00:07:13,015
Point five. Power.

121
00:07:13,335 --> 00:07:15,875
Venus is closer to the Sun,
so above the clouds,

122
00:07:15,955 --> 00:07:18,535
sunlight is almost twice
as strong as on Earth.

123
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:23,169
And the clouds below reflect so much of it
that a solar panel pointing down

124
00:07:23,399 --> 00:07:26,129
would work almost as well
as one pointing up.

125
00:07:26,669 --> 00:07:27,869
But there's a catch.

126
00:07:28,589 --> 00:07:31,749
The planet turns once
every two hundred and forty-three days.

127
00:07:32,289 --> 00:07:33,569
The air does not.

128
00:07:34,193 --> 00:07:37,353
At the height of our city,
the atmosphere races around Venus

129
00:07:37,583 --> 00:07:39,673
in about six or seven days.

130
00:07:40,069 --> 00:07:42,189
And a floating city moves with it.

131
00:07:42,729 --> 00:07:44,789
So night doesn't last twelve hours.

132
00:07:45,109 --> 00:07:46,909
It lasts around three days.

133
00:07:47,635 --> 00:07:50,495
Three days with no sunlight,
running on batteries.

134
00:07:51,415 --> 00:07:54,695
On Mars, a day lasts
twenty-four hours and forty minutes.

135
00:07:55,435 --> 00:07:57,275
Venus three, Mars two.

136
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:01,340
Point six. Water.

137
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:04,440
Venus is one of the driest places we know.

138
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,500
Its atmosphere holds about twenty parts
per million of water vapour.

139
00:08:09,535 --> 00:08:12,735
But there's a twist.
Those droplets aren't pure acid.

140
00:08:13,155 --> 00:08:14,835
They contain water too.

141
00:08:15,415 --> 00:08:19,555
In principle, we could recover it
by processing the sulphuric acid.

142
00:08:20,268 --> 00:08:24,788
It would take a lot of energy, and nobody
has built a machine to do it there.

143
00:08:25,356 --> 00:08:28,556
Mars has water too,
frozen as ice in the ground.

144
00:08:29,196 --> 00:08:30,456
You can dig it up.

145
00:08:31,065 --> 00:08:33,025
Oxygen is the easier part.

146
00:08:33,865 --> 00:08:37,025
In 2021, a small experiment called MOXIE

147
00:08:37,255 --> 00:08:40,905
pulled oxygen straight out of
the carbon dioxide on Mars.

148
00:08:41,637 --> 00:08:43,517
The same trick would work on Venus.

149
00:08:44,237 --> 00:08:46,937
But for water, Mars has the easier source.

150
00:08:47,557 --> 00:08:49,337
Venus three, Mars three.

151
00:08:51,329 --> 00:08:53,269
Point seven. The ground.

152
00:08:53,369 --> 00:08:55,829
On Mars, you can stand on rock.

153
00:08:56,449 --> 00:08:59,489
You can dig, build, and mine
whatever the planet has.

154
00:09:00,134 --> 00:09:04,754
On Venus, everything you build with
has to come from the air, or from Earth.

155
00:09:05,329 --> 00:09:08,529
The surface is right there,
fifty kilometres below.

156
00:09:09,329 --> 00:09:13,069
And nothing we've sent has survived on it
for more than about two hours.

157
00:09:13,768 --> 00:09:16,148
Our balloons lasted about two days.

158
00:09:16,868 --> 00:09:19,948
On Mars, rovers have been driving around
for decades.

159
00:09:20,735 --> 00:09:22,775
Venus three, Mars four.

160
00:09:24,975 --> 00:09:26,795
Point eight. Getting there.

161
00:09:26,875 --> 00:09:29,055
And here, Venus wins again.

162
00:09:29,617 --> 00:09:32,617
Launch windows open
about every year and a half,

163
00:09:32,697 --> 00:09:35,417
instead of every two years or so for Mars.

164
00:09:35,835 --> 00:09:37,575
And a round trip,
with a month spent at Venus,

165
00:09:37,805 --> 00:09:40,115
could take as little as fourteen months.

166
00:09:40,735 --> 00:09:44,015
The same short visit to Mars
takes a year and a half or more.

167
00:09:44,245 --> 00:09:46,055
Four all.

168
00:09:48,205 --> 00:09:50,885
But the score hides
the hardest part of all.

169
00:09:51,265 --> 00:09:52,465
Getting home.

170
00:09:52,911 --> 00:09:57,431
To leave Mars, you launch from the ground,
on a small planet, with weak gravity.

171
00:09:58,191 --> 00:10:01,431
To leave Venus, you launch a rocket
hanging from a balloon,

172
00:10:01,631 --> 00:10:03,951
on a planet almost as heavy as Earth.

173
00:10:04,580 --> 00:10:08,720
To reach orbit there, you have to go
about twice as fast as you would at Mars.

174
00:10:09,476 --> 00:10:12,016
We've never even launched a rocket
from Mars.

175
00:10:12,836 --> 00:10:16,316
It's one of the reasons
bringing back a single rock is so hard.

176
00:10:16,936 --> 00:10:18,816
On Venus, it's harder still.

177
00:10:21,576 --> 00:10:25,176
So that's the real answer.
Mars isn't more Earth-like.

178
00:10:25,256 --> 00:10:27,216
In many ways, Venus is.

179
00:10:27,682 --> 00:10:31,662
But Mars gives you ground to dig,
ice to melt, and a way back home.

180
00:10:32,279 --> 00:10:34,399
Mars offers a place to stand.

181
00:10:35,079 --> 00:10:37,039
Venus offers a place to float.

182
00:10:39,989 --> 00:10:42,549
And maybe one day,
someone will look out of a window

183
00:10:42,779 --> 00:10:44,909
over an endless sea of yellow clouds.

184
00:10:45,739 --> 00:10:50,179
Breathing ordinary air, at room
temperature, in almost ordinary gravity.

185
00:10:50,859 --> 00:10:53,759
With a furnace fifty kilometres
beneath their feet.

186
00:10:56,009 --> 00:10:57,909
So here's a question for you.

187
00:10:58,389 --> 00:11:01,989
Would you rather live in a cave on Mars,
or in a balloon above Venus?

188
00:11:02,789 --> 00:11:04,059
Let me know in the comments.
